  "evidence": {
    "status": "qualified",
    "conditions": "The immutable Linux/ARM64 image ran on four directly connected NVIDIA GB10 systems at TP4/DCP4 with FP8 KV, 24 GiB per rank, a 1,048,576-token request limit, 8,192 scheduler tokens, interval eight, BF16 DFlash2 depth seven, SparkCache read-write access, and two 3 GiB asynchronous capture slots per rank.",
    "measurement": "A deterministic 125,999-token prompt published a reusable boundary. Two retained deep-context entries were restored without either identical prompt being sent after startup. Capture completion, durable commit, restore timing, semantic output, startup inventory, process health, and error logs were recorded on every rank.",
    "result": "The image published 124,928 tokens with capture completion observed in 403.7–408.5 milliseconds and durable commit in 520.6–567.6 milliseconds per rank. It restored 899,072 tokens in 6.47 seconds end-to-end and 999,424 tokens in 6.73 seconds end-to-end. All exact needle checks passed, and all four containers remained healthy.",
    "conclusion": "The DCP4 profile restores persistent context and asynchronously publishes complete snapshots through the recorded 124,928-token and 231.8-MiB-per-rank boundary.",
    "limitations": [
      "DCP1 and DCP2 are implemented profiles, but asynchronous capture is disabled because those slot sizes lack live qualification.",
      "Asynchronous publication above 124,928 stored tokens is not qualified by this evidence.",
      "The public image restored the 900K and 1M entries; the exact earlier writer identity is recorded in the machine receipt.",
      "Page-tail publication, long-duration serving, active-load admission, and concurrent deep-context publication are not qualified."
    ],
